    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This qualitative study explores how activist math teachers in Brazilian educational settings enact alternative teaching practices beyond teaching for the job market and economy. Through qualitative case studies of nine activist teachers, the research investigates how these educators integrate broader social transformation goals into their daily math teaching practices. Drawing on theories of prefiguration and real utopias, the study positions public schools as key sites for contestation and the development of alternative educational practices. Findings reveal that teachers develop alternatives in response to micro-level math oppression, such as math trauma, as well as macro-level social inequalities, by using math as a tool for critical analysis and consciousness-raising. The study highlights various strategies teachers employ, including using social themes as a backdrop for learning mathematical concepts, extracting mathematics from students’ everyday activities, incorporating playful math activities, introducing topics not present in the curriculum, and using concrete materials and experiments. These practices demonstrate the potential of math education to contribute to social transformation and offer insights into actualizing alternatives within state-run institutions.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Rocha Bueno, M. (2024). Another Math is Possible: Alternatives in Math Education from Teacher Activists in Brazilian Public Schools. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/zw381np3356. https://doi.org/10.25740/zw381np3356.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
